      "content": "The Court of Appeal in Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd (2023) reaffirmed the principle that proximity must be both physical and legal for a duty of care to extend to sub-contracted employees under the doctrine of vicarious proximity. This principle, established in Duncombe v. Northern Rail (2014), has been refined through subsequent cases, including Henderson v. Global Shipping (2020), which clarified the 'control' aspect of the control test in Smith v. Jones (2018). The court's decision in Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd (2023) built upon this refinement, finding that the level of autonomy granted to sub-contractors precluded a finding of vicarious liability, consistent with the precedent in Miller v. Transport Corp (2019). The court's rejection of the control test in Smith v. Jones (2018) also aligns with the approach taken in Duncombe v. Northern Rail (2014), which emphasized the importance of proximity in determining the scope of liability. Furthermore, the decision in Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd (2023) is consistent with the broader trend in English law, which has increasingly recognized the need to balance the competing interests of autonomy and liability in the context of vic",
